WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.450
- This episode of Legal Eagle

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:03.120
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.580
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.300
by clicking on the link
in the description.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.590
Authorities say that the
El Dorado forest fire

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.550
that is currently burning
out of control in California

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.500
was started at a gender reveal
party when a couple detonated

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.380
a smoke generating pyrotechnic device

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.840
to announce to the world that
their unborn baby was a son.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.680
The device ignited the
four foot tall grass

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.250
of the El Dorado Ranch Park

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.230
on a day when most of
California was being roasted

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.460
by temperatures over a hundred degrees.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.640
So far thousands, if not millions of acres

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.580
have burned allegedly,

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.550
as a result of this gender reveal party.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.620
Hundreds of firefighters
are battling the blaze

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.930
and thousands of people
have had to evacuate.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.650
So congratulations, it's a fire.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.960
And because we live in
the dumbest timeline,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.600
gender reveal parties
have actually sparked

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.900
at least four disasters,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.050
including two forest fires, a plane crash

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.270
and a person killed by what
was effectively a pipe bomb.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.500
Seriously, why do people do this?

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.810
Why do they think it's a good idea?

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.460
But this raises a
different legal question.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.990
Shouldn't the people whose
actions start wildfires

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.420
be held responsible for the
damages to life and property?

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.280
Well, the El Dorado blaze
was just one of several fires

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.970
that continued to burn up
and down the West Coast

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.860
as extreme heat created
hazardous conditions.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.040
And therein lies the
first of many problems

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.190
in assigning blame and recouping costs.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.430
Here, allegedly, a couple started a fire,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.620
but how do you decide
if they have liability?

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.860
And if so, when millions of acres burn,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.480
where does their liability end?

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.630
And who normally pays for
damages caused by massive fires?

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.710
And finally, if billion
dollar fires are more common

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:33.840
because of climate change

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.313
how will the law rise
to meet this challenge?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.980
Hey, Legal Eagles, It's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.020
because we didn't start the fire,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.830
it was always burning since
the world's been turning.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.080
But thanks to climate change

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.870
it's burning a whole lot more often.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.040
And in that situation
who pays for the damages

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.370
caused by these massive, massive wildfires

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.740
that are currently burning
California and Oregon?

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.270
And in general, there are
probably three main sources

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.960
that are likely to provide compensation

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.920
in a situation like
this with forest fires.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.470
One is insurance companies,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.820
though that's increasingly
becoming less likely

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.290
to pay for circumstances like these.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.480
Two is the federal government.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.520
In other words, taxpayers
are likely to pick up the bag

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.930
when insurance companies refuse to.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.130
And then third, of
course, are other people,

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.920
people that are responsible for the fires

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.700
or people that contribute to the fires

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.220
and potentially people who
contribute to global warming.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.680
Well fires are as old
as civilization itself.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.130
And as a result we've developed
a number of legal tools

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.160
to help deal with the
liability that's caused

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.450
from those conflagrations.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.820
And if you know your American history,

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.000
you might recall that Mrs
O'Leary and her cow were blamed

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.190
for the great Chicago fire of 1871,

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.990
which killed 120 people,

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.450
consumed over three square
miles of the city center

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.880
and caused $200 million in
damages in today's money.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.260
Eventually historians concluded

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.110
that she was the victim of
anti-immigration sentiment

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.550
and the city formally
exonerated her in 1997.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.340
But similarly, in 2017, Rita
Yeboah, a Ghanian immigrant,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.790
was blamed for the deadly
Bronx apartment fire

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.480
that killed 13 people.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.340
Her three year old son
was playing with the oven

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.700
when it caught fire and
Yeboah was chastised

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.860
by local authorities for
not attending to her son

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.510
and failing to close the door.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.180
These two stories have more in common

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.800
than just anti-immigration sentiment.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.900
There's no doubt that this
Chicago fire was started

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.320
in Mrs. O'Leary's barn just
like there was no question

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.070
that the deadly Bronx fire was started

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.430
in Yeboah's apartment,

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.300
but Mrs. O'Leary disputed the idea

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.040
that she had negligently milked a cow

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.830
which allegedly kicked over a
lantern and started the blaze.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.570
And similarly, Yeboah fought
back by suing her landlord

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:32.990
for permitting dangerous conditions

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:33.990
in the apartment building

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.480
which may have had doors
that wouldn't stay closed.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.243
Although both women social stigma,

00:03:38.243 --> 00:03:40.880
neither of them had to pay
the damages to property

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.640
or the cost of fire suppression.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.050
And that's because in America,

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.150
fires that weren't
intentionally set by arsonists

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.370
have historically been
treated as, well, accidents.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.640
However, in 2020,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.400
massive wildfires are
frighteningly commonplace

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.180
and they're taxing our traditional methods

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.420
of paying for them.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.160
And we'll get to the personal liability

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.180
that stems from causing fires,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.920
as in what happens when you
get sued for causing a fire

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:03.850
in just a second.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.100
But let's start with
the more traditional way

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.590
that people get
compensation for accidents,

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.900
especially when it comes
to homes and fires,

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.980
which is the insurance industry.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.250
And for those who are
uninsured or underinsured,

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.010
FEMA offers individual and
household disaster assistance

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.670
that can help pay for damages
to homes and businesses.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.270
Generally, we're talking about
standard homeowners policies

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.360
the insurance policy that everyone needs

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.370
when you buy our own house.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.540
Which generally covers
destruction and damage

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.660
caused by a fire, which
generally includes wildfires.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.550
In the event of a fire,

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.750
the insurance company will
pay to rebuild or repair

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.960
an insured home and
remediate smoke damage.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.980
This includes the
outbuildings on the property

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.790
such as a garage or a tool shed.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.110
Some insurance policies
cover theft or vandalism

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.730
that happens in the event of looting

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.500
in the wake of a wildfire.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.210
Businesses tend to have their own policies

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.330
which cover fire damage to the building,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.040
office space and equipment and inventory.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.010
And of course, insurance
is an important piece

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.180
of the fire cost puzzle,
but there are limits.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.610
In this case, literally.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.680
Because the amount of the insurance payout

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.490
is determined by the terms and
limits of the policy itself.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.470
Generally, homeowners
policies don't cover limits

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.100
beyond one to $2 million

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.200
unless the homeowner has
paid for more coverage.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.150
And every policy carries exclusions

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.500
that prevent the insurance
company from having a payout,

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.480
if the situation falls
under one of those scenarios

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:17.820
covered by the exclusion.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.110
And whenever you have this
much property destruction

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:21.620
at exactly the same time,

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.330
the insurance industry is notorious

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.970
for doing whatever it
can to avoid paying out.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.160
As one of my former law
partners used to say,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.980
the only thing an insurance
policy is good for

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.670
is getting the right to
sue your insurance company.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.180
Already, these wildfires
in Oregon and California

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.830
are causing huge losses
in the insurance industry.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.620
And a few years ago, back in 2018,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:42.300
the campfire fire was
the costliest wildfire

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.360
in California history,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.110
causing more than 14,000
structures to be destroyed.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.620
And the insurance industry had
losses of over $14 billion.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.260
The scale of those losses,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.760
in other words the insurance
companies had to pay out

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.090
billions of dollars to the people

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:57.600
that bought insurance from them,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.150
prompted reinsurance
companies to step back.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.830
Now re-insurance is insurance

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.370
purchased by insurance companies

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.660
that are offloading some
of the financial risks

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.430
of a major catastrophe.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:08.870
The availability of re-insurance

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.010
enables better known primary carers,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.710
such as farmers and state farm

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.510
to keep writing coverage in areas

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.110
that are most likely to
experience wildfires.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.080
Now, while primary insurance companies

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.030
generally face tons of
regulations that prevent them

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.600
from raising prices for high risk areas,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.940
reinsurance companies generally

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:27.620
are not subject to rate regulation

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.780
by the California Department of Insurance.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:32.180
As a result, re-insurance
rates are skyrocketing

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:33.970
by as much as 70%.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:37.220
So since insurers can't raise
homeowners insurance rates

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.500
because of state regulations
to match the actual price

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.410
of the things that
they're having to pay out

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.840
because of the wildfires,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.750
generally because of state regulations,

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.290
insurance companies have
reduced the number of policies

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:49.320
that they underwrite.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.310
And some people living in rural California

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.830
now have properties that
are effectively uninsurable.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.860
So, while many homeowners
in affected areas

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.880
may be able to get an
insurance payout this year,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.050
effectively the insurance companies

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.370
may leave the area entirely
because it's too risky.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.030
And the housing markets
in these areas may tank

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.070
because no one's going to
buy an uninsured house,

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.680
especially in an area that's
going to be facing wildfires

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:12.770
year after year.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.150
People love to dunk on insurance
companies, myself included,

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:19.210
but if it becomes too costly
to provide that insurance,

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:20.470
the insurance company will just simply

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.000
leave the market entirely.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.720
But with insurance companies
increasingly refusing

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.090
to provide coverage for
these kinds of areas,

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.870
that might mean that
the federal government,

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:29.950
in other words taxpayers,

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:31.480
are going to have to pick up the bill.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.550
And when it comes to the
cost of finding the fires

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.540
the federal government
has stepped in to cover

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:36.920
some of those costs.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.650
The Trump administration issued
a major disaster declaration

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.200
for California wildfires,
which opened up the gates

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.990
for federal funding to be
funneled into recovery efforts.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.650
Funding through the
declarations covers up to 75%

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.750
of the costs incurred by
California for removing debris,

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.550
conducting emergency activities,

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:54.180
providing transitional sheltering

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.580
and presumably raking the forest

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.900
as the president has told
us that we should be doing.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.817
- I was with the president
of Finland and he said:

00:08:01.817 --> 00:08:05.210
"We have a much different.
We're a forest nation."

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.067
He called it a forest nation.

00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:10.250
And they spent a lot of
time on raking and cleaning

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.380
and doing things and they
don't have any problem.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.250
And what it is, it's a very small problem.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:17.360
We've gotta take care
of the floors, you know?

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.050
The floors of the forest, very important.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:21.630
- And federal funding is also available

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:22.940
on a cost sharing basis

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.130
for hazard mitigation measures statewide.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.700
Now the federal government
has spent about $1.58 billion

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.760
on fire suppression in 2019.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.840
And this is more than triple
the amount allocated in 2018.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.740
Unfortunately, some of the
money that was allocated

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:39.740
to fight fires was shifted from
fire prevention activities.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.520
And the West coast fires
are often politicized

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.060
in a really ugly way.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:47.080
Now there is a source of
quasi governmental entities

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.670
that might provide compensation

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:50.540
and that would be the utility companies.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.980
Now, most experts say that
extreme weather conditions

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.160
that lead to fires are
caused by climate change.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.900
But the laws that we have on the books

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:58.400
just haven't caught up with the scale

00:08:58.400 --> 00:08:59.630
of these massive fires.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.700
This is a big problem for
state and local governments,

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.060
which are left footing bill

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.100
for the cost of fire
suppression and cleanup.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.240
And sometimes there's
an easy, wealthy target

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:08.240
for the state to go after.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.450
For example, California's
department of forestry

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.890
and fire protection determined
that 12 devastated fires

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:15.570
that struck Northern California in 2017

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.190
were the result of trees coming
in contact with power lines

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.700
or other failures by the
utility company, PG&amp;E.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.940
And thanks to a policy known
as inverse condemnation,

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.960
the utility has to pay those damages

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.030
which the company believes
will be over $10 billion.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.960
And even if the company is
found to be not negligent.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:33.780
Inverse condemnation is a legal doctrine

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:34.900
that holds utility companies,

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.120
like PG&amp;E, Pacific Gas and Electric

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.060
to a strict liability standard.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.020
Under strict liability,

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.910
it doesn't matter if fire starts
by accident or negligence.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.050
If it happens on the company's equipment

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.600
then the utility is
responsible for those damages.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.040
The California court decisions
in the 1960s and seventies

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.330
allowed the state to
use inverse condemnation

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.920
to hold utilities liable.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:54.753
For many years,

00:09:54.753 --> 00:09:56.840
the utility companies
could just handle the costs

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.740
by increasing the rates
charged to consumers.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.560
But in 2017, the California
Public Utilities Commission

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.810
turned down requests by
SDG&amp;E for $379 million

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.960
in cost reimbursements
for the 2007 wildfires

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.850
sparked by their lines.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.530
Now power companies may be
on the hook for actual costs

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.940
without electricity hikes.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.530
And how they're going to
do that is anyone's guess.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.790
PG&amp;E filed for bankruptcy protection.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:22.720
It argued that the disallowance
amounted to a violation

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.150
of the Takings Clause
of the Fifth Amendment

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.650
of the United States constitution

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.330
and the California constitution.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.070
Legal theory is that even
though the state did not take

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:32.810
any PG&amp;E property, applying
inverse condemnation

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.120
to the company effectively
transfers the company

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.010
to the state without just compensation

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:39.320
or any compensation at all.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.210
And the company argued
that if it was allowed to

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.570
pass the costs to consumers
through increased utility rates,

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.880
inverse condemnation would
be costly institutional.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.300
But a federal bankruptcy
court rejected this argument

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.310
and the case is on appeal in front of

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:52.730
the California Supreme court.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.270
Which takes us to the next
it's a group of people

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.500
who are likely to face some liability.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.600
And that is of course, the
tort liability of individuals.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.830
A tort is a wrong committed by a person

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:05.020
that ends up causing harm to
someone or something else.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:07.210
The tort at stake in wildfire cases

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:08.700
is most likely negligence.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.850
And Black's Law Dictionary
defines negligence

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.520
as the failure to exercise
the standard of care

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.980
that a reasonably prudent
person would have exercised

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.670
in a similar situation.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.990
And there are four elements
in a negligence claim.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:23.610
Duty, a breach of that
duty, injury and causation.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.150
The two elements that I want to highlight

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:26.950
in a wildfire negligence case

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.880
is the duty of care and causation,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.400
because damages are pretty obvious here.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.840
Now the duty of care is based
on what a reasonable person

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.390
in the same or similar
circumstance would do.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:39.000
It's an objective test based
on what a reasonable person

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.440
would have done in the same
or similar circumstances.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:44.020
The circumstances of the El
Dorado gender reveal party

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:45.930
help us to define whether a person acted

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.560
reasonably or negligently.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:50.140
Did the people detonate
explosives in their backyard,

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:52.110
near a pool or were they in an area

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.110
with high flammable dry foliage?

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:55.350
What was the weather like?

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.390
Was it hot and dry or cool and rainy?

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.180
Had local authorities warned people

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.450
that conditions were dangerous

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.090
and could potentially lead to fire?

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:04.330
The facts determined whether
people acted reasonably

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.450
or like idiots.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.670
I mean, acted reasonably.

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.960
And as I've said before, if
you ask a lawyer anything,

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.870
the answer is almost always, it depends.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.160
And I try not to take
a stand on most things,

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.240
but I will go out on a limb and say that

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.330
if you are detonating a bomb

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:21.420
in front of a bunch of dry
grass on a hundred degree day,

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.410
that's probably negligence.

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.060
But that takes us to the
second element of negligence

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:27.590
that's at play here.

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:28.740
And that's causation.

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.700
Now, there are two types
of negligent causation,

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.440
there's actual cause and proximate cause.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.250
And actual cause is sometimes
referred to as cause-in-fact.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:37.670
It means that but for the negligent act

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.530
or the omission by the defendant,

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.320
the plaintiff would not have been harmed.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:43.430
This is known as the but for test.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.580
For example, if a couple detonates a bomb

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.050
in front of a bunch of dry grass

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:50.810
on an incredibly hot dry day,
and that grass catches fire

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:53.080
and sets their neighbor's house on fire,

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.920
then it's clear that the
but for test is made.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.920
But for the bomb going off and
the fire consuming the house,

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:01.750
the house wouldn't have been damaged.

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:03.540
But the second type of negligent causation

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:04.550
is called proximate cause.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.340
And approximate cause requires
that the natural direct

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:10.570
and uninterrupted consequence
of a negligent act or omission

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:12.660
causes the plaintiff's injury.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:14.400
For example, the El
Dorado couple's actions

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.420
did start a huge fire but
where does their liability end?

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:19.690
What if once the fire had started,

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:22.030
Joe Rogan, seeing the fire spreading,

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.030
decided that he wanted
to create a copycat fire

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:28.550
and blame liberal activists
for setting fires?

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:29.383
I'm a little salty

00:13:29.383 --> 00:13:31.630
because Joe Rogan is
blaming liberal activists

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.460
for the fires, which is insane.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.420
- I said something on the
podcast with Douglas Murray

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.260
about people getting
arrested for lighting fires.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.290
You know, air quote,
activists. And I got duped.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:46.530
- Obviously, the El
Dorado couple's actions

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:47.740
are a but for cause.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:51.270
Seeing the fires led Joe
Rogan to start a copycat fire.

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:53.760
But it's hard to say that their actions

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.530
were the proximate
cause of the second fire

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:58.380
spreading and causing damage.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.520
And extending this
hypothetical even further,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.740
what happens if the first fire
and the second fire combined

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:06.850
to form a super fire and
then damage other property?

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.530
Who's then responsible
for the damages caused

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:10.930
by the combined fire?

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:12.600
It's hard to say, that's the problem

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.160
of proving proximate cause.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.430
But back to the real world,

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:16.860
I think a jury could be persuaded

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:18.710
that the El Dorado
couple acted negligently

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:19.980
and caused property damages.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.920
But collecting many billions
of dollars from that couple

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:26.100
would be a daunting,
if not impossible task,

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:28.190
they're not Jeff Basos.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:29.960
But just because someone
might be judgment proof

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.890
and can't afford the billions
of dollars of property damage

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:35.810
doesn't mean that they're
going to escape all penalties.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.130
And there are other options
for local government.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.890
With fires on the rise in the West,

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:41.330
local and state governments have started

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.250
going after people for starting fires.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:45.430
In Oregon, for example,
two men were billed

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.360
for starting a fire
that burned 26,000 acres

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.010
over the course of a month.

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.460
And the men were billed
for $37 million in damages.

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.650
They both did used lawn
mowers to mow grass

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.830
during the daytime, which
was prohibited at the time

00:14:57.830 --> 00:14:59.500
because the conditions were so hot and dry

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:02.090
that they could cause
a fire, which they did.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.310
But under Oregon law, the
men could only be fined

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:05.810
in accordance with civil penalties.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.300
They were each find $550 each

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:09.570
for a fire that cost it's a state

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.010
at least $37 million to suppress.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:13.980
But California law
provides an additional way

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.680
to penalize reckless property owners.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:18.590
For example, in 2016,
the U.S. Forest Service

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:19.960
sued a California property owner

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.830
seeking almost $25 million in compensation

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.180
for the fire that burned
15,000 acres of federal lands

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:28.280
in San Bernardino National Forest.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.440
Investigators determined
that the fire ignited

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:32.120
in a plastic electrical junction box

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.740
that wasn't properly sealed.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:36.380
An electrical discharge inside
the book box shots sparks

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.990
onto dry ground vegetation,
which sparked a mountain fire.

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.920
The theories of liability
included negligence,

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.140
trespass by fire and
violations of California's

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.900
Health and Safety Code Section 13001,

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.530
which allows the state to pass
the cost of fire suppression

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.440
to any person who
negligently sparks a fire.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.730
Obviously the plaintiff is
unlikely to get $25 million

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:57.620
but the suit allows the
government to negotiate

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.080
with an insurance company,
provided the property was insured

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.420
and sends a message that
they hope will deter others

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:03.800
from similar conduct.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.490
Of course, if the civil fines
and lawsuits aren't enough

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:09.520
a person can be prosecuted
criminally as well.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:10.950
A person can be prosecuted

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.280
for reckless or extremely
negligent conduct.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.800
The kind of thing that we would
normally call an accident.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:18.420
Under California Penal Code Section 452,

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:20.980
it's a crime for a person
to recklessly set a fire

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.170
or burn any structure,
forest, land, or property.

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:26.050
California Penal Code Section 452

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:28.620
makes it a crime for a person
to recklessly set a fire

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:31.750
or to burn any structure,
forest, land or property.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.710
A person acts recklessly
if the person is aware

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.350
that their actions could present

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:39.490
a substantial and unjustifiable
risk of causing a fire.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:40.690
The person ignores that risk

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:42.900
and ignoring the risk is a gross deviation

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.640
from how a reasonable person would act

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.010
in the same situation.

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.060
A reckless action is a higher standard

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.140
than mistakes or even negligence.

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:51.550
Negligent acts include things,

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:54.200
like forgetting to turn
off the burner on a stove.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:55.670
This alone won't subject someone

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:56.950
to a reckless burning charge.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.960
However, acting with complete
disregard for safety may.

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:03.320
So for example, if you were
here or trying to heat up

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:05.970
a can of gasoline on the stove,

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:08.010
that might qualify as recklessness.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:10.020
You didn't intend for a fire to start,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.990
but you should know that if
you're heating up gasoline

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:15.750
on a stove on an open flame,
a fire may result from that.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.900
And basic reckless burning
of personal property

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:20.320
under Penal Code Section
452 is a misdemeanor

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:22.550
with a maximum penalty
of six months in jail

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.560
and a fine up to a thousand dollars.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:26.240
But if the reckless burning results

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:28.190
in a burned structure or forest land,

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.210
or if it causes great bodily injury

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:31.700
then the crime may be prosecuted

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:33.430
as either a misdemeanor or a felony.

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:35.320
And that's what happened to William Rupp

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.780
who served two years in
prison recklessly causing

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:39.760
the Lake Shasta fire in 2008.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.180
The 56-year-old was accused

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:42.850
of intentionally ignoring in warnings

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.350
of a potential fire hazard
by cutting the grass

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:47.550
on his rural property
on an extremely dry day

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:50.040
when temperatures were about 106 degrees.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.080
A mower blade struck a rock,
sparked and ignited the fire

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:56.530
which burned 86 rural
homes at around 1:30 p.m.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:59.150
Prosecutors said Rupp
ignored warnings not to mow

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:01.760
from neighbors and public
service spots on TV

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.710
and told one person who
questioned him to go to hell.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.670
And a similar situation
happened in Arizona

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.320
when an off duty border patrol agent

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:11.940
was ordered to pay $220,000 in restitution

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.930
after he started at 47,000 acre wildfire

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:16.440
at a gender reveal party.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.130
Again with the frigging
gender reveal parties.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:21.200
In that case, Dennis Dickey pleaded guilty

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:24.520
to the misdemeanor charge of
causing a fire without a permit

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:28.440
after he caused the April,
2017 sawmill fire in Arizona.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:31.130
Dickey reportedly shot a
target containing tannerite,

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:32.590
which is an explosive substance

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:35.870
designed to detonate when shot
by a high velocity firearm.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:38.500
And colored powder that would
reveal the baby's gender

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:39.950
at the party.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:43.040
Tannerite has reportedly
been linked to past wildfires

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.710
and featured prominently in Tiger King.

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:49.880
- Are you ready for
this? (mimics gunshots)

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:53.150
(gunshots firing)

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:54.180
Oh, hell!

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:56.680
- Dickey of course claimed
it was a complete accident

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:58.940
and that he immediately
contacted authorities.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.300
And that's probably true,

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:01.720
but the problem with claiming

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:02.880
that it's a complete accident

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.860
is that there were 40 mile an hour winds

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.310
that day in the desert in an area

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.890
that had already experienced
a drought conditions.

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:11.260
And you would think that a
reasonable person would know

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.380
that a high explosive in dry grass

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:18.070
with fast winds is likely
to cause a really big fire.

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:19.960
But if you're throwing
a gender reveal party

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.340
